  <h1>Classic VIN decoder: methodology and sources</h1>
  <p class="lead">Last reviewed October 22, 2026. Our rule is simple: we decode a character only when its meaning for that maker, model and year is documented in a source we can cite. Everything else is shown as a raw code marked “Can’t confirm.”</p>

  <h2>Why classic VINs need a different decoder</h2>
  <p>The 17-character VIN format, with its check digit, is set by federal regulation (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-565" target="_blank" rel="noopener">49 CFR Part 565</a>) and applies from the 1981 model year. Car Alpha decodes those VINs through NHTSA’s free <a href="https://vpic.nhtsa.dot.gov/api/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vPIC API</a>. Earlier cars used manufacturer-specific serial numbers with no check digit, so vPIC can’t read them. The classic decoder runs entirely in your browser from the tables below.</p>

  <h2>How we detect the layout</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>GM, 1965–1971 (13 characters, all digits except the plant):</strong> division, two-digit series, two-digit body style, year digit, plant letter, six-digit sequence.</li>
    <li><strong>GM, 1972–1980 (13 characters):</strong> division, series letter, two-digit body, engine character, year (2–9, or A for 1980), plant, sequence.</li>
    <li><strong>Ford (11 characters):</strong> year digit, plant letter, two-digit body serial code, engine letter, six-digit consecutive unit number.</li>
    <li><strong>Chrysler Corporation (13 characters):</strong> car line, price class, two-digit body type, engine, year digit, plant, six-digit sequence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A number that doesn’t fit one of these patterns isn’t decoded. We say so rather than guess.</p>

  <h2>Confidence levels</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Documented:</strong> the code’s meaning is stated in a cited reference for that year and line.</li>
    <li><strong>Likely:</strong> the code matches a cited table, but the serial alone can’t rule out another reading. Examples: Ford’s year digit repeats every decade; a Chrysler year digit of 8, 9 or 0 could also be 1978–80; plant letters taken from a Chevrolet-wide table; 1970 Chrysler engine letters taken from club registries rather than the factory bulletin.</li>
    <li><strong>Can’t confirm:</strong> no cited table covers the code. We show the raw character.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Automatic sanity checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>1967–69 Camaro sequence numbers below that year’s documented starting number (100001, 300001, 500001).</li>
    <li>1970 Charger built anywhere but St. Louis (plant G).</li>
    <li>Engine letters that aren’t valid for the decoded year.</li>
    <li>A reminder to match the VIN to the trim or fender tag and the engine and transmission stampings on Hemi, 440 and other often-cloned cars.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pre-1965 GM, pre-1968 Chrysler and most non-Mustang Fords aren’t fully decoded yet.</li>
    <li>For 1965–71 GM cars the VIN doesn’t identify the exact engine or packages like SS, Z/28 or RS.</li>
    <li>VIN decoding can’t verify authenticity, title status or condition.</li>
    <li>Canadian-built cars sometimes used different sequence schemes.</li>
  </ul>

